{"name":"Clambake","hex":"#e0d1bb","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"clambake","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An informal beach party in which food, usually seafood, is cooked in a firepit dug in the sand, filled with hot coals.","An instance of smoking (usually marijuana) in an enclosed space."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Informal, makeshift, sloppy."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/clambake"}],"entries":[{"title":"Clambake","blocks":[{"text":"The clambake or clam bake, also known as the New England clambake, is a traditional method of cooking seafood, such as lobster, mussels, crabs, scallops, soft-shell clams, and quahogs. The food is traditionally cooked by steaming the ingredients over layers of seaweed in a pit oven. The shellfish can be supplemented with vegetables, such as onions, carrots, and corn on the cob. Clambakes are usually held on festive occasions along the coast of New England and at fundraisers and political events. Some restaurants and caterers offer clambake-style food."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d7/New_England_clam_bake.jpg/330px-New_England_clam_bake.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Clambake","wikidata":"Q7007443"},{"title":"Clambake (soundtrack)","blocks":[{"text":"Clambake is the sixteenth soundtrack album by American singer and musician Elvis Presley, released by RCA Victor in mono and stereo, LPM/LSP 3893, in October 1967. It is the soundtrack to the 1967 film of the same name starring Presley. He entered RCA Studio B in Nashville, Tennessee on February 21, 1967, for recording sessions for his twenty-fifth film. Supplemental material sessions took place on September 10 and 11, 1967. It peaked at number 40 on the Billboard 200."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/e/ef/Elvis_Presley_Clambake_LP_Cover.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Clambake_(soundtrack)","wikidata":"Q181038"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/73/Beach_clambake.png/330px-Beach_clambake.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/73/Beach_clambake.png/330px-Beach_clambake.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Illustration of a pit being dug in the beach sand in preparation for a traditional clambake, 1907","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABeach_clambake.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f4/RYE_3639.jpg/500px-RYE_3639.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f4/RYE_3639.jpg/1280px-RYE_3639.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f4/RYE_3639.jpg/500px-RYE_3639.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Clambake from Philadelphia","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARYE_3639.jpg"}],"sounds":{"clambake":[{"type":"audio/wav","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/4a/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Flame%2C_not_lame-clambake.wav?utm_source=en.wiktionary.org&utm_campaign=rest&utm_content=original"},{"type":"audio/mpeg","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/4/4a/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Flame%2C_not_lame-clambake.wav/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Flame%2C_not_lame-clambake.wav.mp3"},{"type":"audio/ogg; codecs=\"vorbis\"","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/4/4a/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Flame%2C_not_lame-clambake.wav/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Flame%2C_not_lame-clambake.wav.ogg"}]},"pictures":[{"title":"Grey heron (00129p)","image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons